<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esarhaddon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 681–669 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esarhaddon was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> in the seventh century BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and in 671 BCE invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, driving out the Kushite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> and capturing Memphis, a defeat that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>'s loss of Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esarhaddon.jpg" alt="Esarhaddon" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail._Sam%27al_stele_of_Esarhaddon,_671_BCE,_Pergamon_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Esarhaddon was the third king of the Sargonid dynasty, and his name in Akkadian, Aššur-aḫa-iddina, means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-god">Ashur</a> has given me a brother. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s first choice of heir was his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nadin-shumi">Ashur-nadin-shumi</a>, whom he made ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> around 700 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> captured the prince at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> in 694 BCE and probably executed him. Sennacherib then raised his second surviving son, Arda-Mulissu, to crown prince, but in 684 BCE replaced him with Esarhaddon, a younger son. The reason is unknown, but Arda-Mulissu was deeply disappointed. Esarhaddon later wrote that his brothers went mad and drew their swords in the middle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On about 20 October 681 BCE Arda-Mulissu and another brother, Nabû-šarru-uṣur, killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> in a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. Their supporters fell out, delaying a coronation, and Esarhaddon raised an army and met his brothers' forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanigalbat">Hanigalbat</a>, where most of the soldiers deserted to him. He then marched on Nineveh unopposed, after a six-week war of succession. His brothers' attempt left him distrustful of officials, governors and male relatives, and he often sought the advice of oracles and priests. His mother Naqiʾa, his wife Esharra-hammat and his daughter Šērūʾa-ēṭirat held more influence than women had in earlier Assyrian history.</p><p>He sponsored building and restoration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> on a far greater scale than any predecessor, probably to show the benefits of Assyrian rule. He faced Urartu under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-ii">Rusa II</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, and after defeating the Median kings Eparna and Shidirparna many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. His first invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, in 673 BCE, failed, and his health was deteriorating.</p><p>In 671 BCE he marched again with a much larger army, and a prophecy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> predicted success. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. A conspiracy followed in 671-670 BCE, and he performed the substitute king ritual to avert evil. In 669 BCE Egypt rebelled, and Esarhaddon died at Harran in about November 669 BCE, apparently of natural causes, on his way to campaign against it a third time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Esarhaddon arranged for his sons to succeed him peacefully, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> as ruler of the empire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin">Shamash-shum-ukin</a> as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Ashurbanipal finished the campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 667 BCE. Historians generally regard Esarhaddon as gentler than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> and as one of the most successful Assyrian kings, and according to the Assyriologist Karen Radner he emerges more clearly as an individual than any other Assyrian king because court correspondence from his reign survives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Esarhaddon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Assyrian cuneiform inscriptions call the land Údumi or Údumu and name three of its kings: Kaus-malaka in the time of Tiglath-pileser III (c. 745 BCE), Aya-ramu in that of Sennacherib (c. 705 BCE) and Kaus-gabri in that of <b>Esarhaddon</b> (c. 680 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Esarhaddon</b> took Memphis in 671 BCE, and after Taharqa's attempts to recover Egypt, Ashurbanipal's army sacked Thebes in 663 BCE, ending the rule of Tantamani in Egypt and forcing a retreat to Nubia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>To their enemies the Cimmerians were outsiders who did not fear the Mesopotamian gods; <b>Esarhaddon</b>'s adviser Bēl-ušēzib warned the king to spy on them.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In 701 BCE Sennacherib’s advance drove the king of Sidon to flee overseas, and in 677 BCE <b>Esarhaddon</b> captured rebellious Sidon and deported its people; its king, Abdi-Milkutti, escaped by sea but was caught and executed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scythians enter written history in Assyrian texts of the 670s BCE, when King <b>Esarhaddon</b> asked the sun god whether he should give a daughter in marriage to Bartatua, king of the Ishkuzai, usually identified with Herodotus’s Protothyes.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 671 BCE, when <b>Esarhaddon</b> conquered Egypt, Assyria was the greatest power the world had yet seen, and the Institute's curve stands at its peak.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.87,39.03&amp;year=-675" aria-label="Harran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5529 835 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Esarhaddon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5979 1105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5979 1105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.87,39.03&amp;year=-675">What was here in 675 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
